<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Samara Bend</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1223">1223</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-invasion-of-volga-bulgaria">Mongol invasion of Volga Bulgaria</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Samara Bend was fought in 1223 near the great bend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> at Samara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, when the Volga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> ambushed part of the Mongol army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> returning from its victory on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalka">Kalka</a> River. Medieval sources report heavy Mongol losses. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> returned under Batu and conquered Volga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 1236.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1221 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">tumens</a>, 20,000 men, to reconnoitre the lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, on condition that the campaign take no more than two years and that on the way back they join his son Jochi against the Volga Bulgars. The two generals defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iv-of-georgia">George IV of Georgia</a>, crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> and their allies, and on the Kalka River destroyed a combined Cuman and Rus army in 1223 before turning east.</p><p>The Volga Bulgars, apparently of Turkic origin, had settled in the Volga-Kama region and embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in 922, becoming a trading centre between the Islamic world and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In the 12th century the principality of Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a>, expanding on the middle Volga, had taken several of their towns, including Osel, and forced them to move their capital to Bilar, leaving the state weakened.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>A messenger from the khan ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> to find Jochi on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and return with him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. Jochi brought a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">tumen</a> of 10,000 men, and the army subdued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> on the west bank of the river before moving further north. The only record of what followed is a short passage by the Muslim historian Ibn al-Athir, writing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. In it the Bulgars laid ambushes in several places, drew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> past them and attacked from behind, killing most of them. A second version in the same chronicle mentions no encounter with the Bulgars at all. The historian A. H. Halikov names the Bulgar commander as Ilgam Khan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Historians disagree about the battle. Morgan, Chambers and Grousset hold that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in fact defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>, and Chambers suggests the Bulgars made up the story of their victory. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-jackson">Peter Jackson</a>'s reading, the figure of about four thousand in Ibn al-Athir's second version refers to the size of the whole Mongol army, not to survivors. Afterwards the Mongols skirted the Urals, defeated the Saxin and the Kanglis and took a large tribute, which would hardly have been possible had their force been badly damaged. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> died of a fever on the Imil River on the way home.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Samara Bend” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1223 the Bulgars ambushed and defeated part of the Mongol army of Subutai and Jebe near the <b>Samara Bend</b> as it returned from the Battle of the Kalka River.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Military</a></li></ul></section>
